\title{The disjunctive property}
\taxon{Module}
\author{reidbarton}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\import{stt-macros}
\date{2026-06-08}
\date{2026-06-09}
\date{2026-09-14}

\subtree[stt-011B]{
\taxon{Idea}
\author{fredrikbakke}
\contributor{reidbarton}
\date{2026-09-14}

\p{
Given a lattice #{\II}, the \em{disjunctive property} states that if #{i \lor j = 1}
for any #{i, j : \II} then #{i = 1} or #{j = 1}. We show that if
\ol{
\li{The boundary inclusion of #{\II} is #{♭}-surjective.}
\li{Types with initial objects separate.}
\li{
The interval is [directed](Synthetic.Directedness), in the sense that any endomap on
#{\II} is monotone.
}
}
then #{\II} satisfies the disjunctive property.
}

\p{
The above listed conditions generalise the following respective axioms from
Triangulated Type Theory \citek{GWB24}:
\ol{
\li{There are precisely two crisp elements of #{\II}. \citet{Axiom 7}{GWB24}}
\li{Finite-dimensional cubes separate. \citet{Axiom 8}{GWB24}}
\li{The interval satisfies [duality](Synthetic.Quasicoherence). \citet{Axiom 10}{GWB24}}
}
}

\remark{
\p{
The disjunctive property for flat distributive lattices is proved externally by
[[basspitters]] in \citet{Lemma 2}{Spi16}. Here we derive the property internally
instead.
}
}
}

\agda{
open import Foundations.Universes

open import Algebra.Lattice
